{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>In the most holy place you shall eat it</b> — This teaches concerning most holy sacrifices (קדשי קדשים) that they may be eaten only in the courtyard and by males of the priesthood; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 18:10). But I do not understand this, for \"the most holy place\" (קדש הקדשים) is not the place of eating of the sacrifices, since Scripture says: \"In a holy place it shall be eaten, in the court of the Tent of Meeting\" (Leviticus 6:19), and the most holy place is in the location of the Ark, inside the curtain (פרוכת), as it is written: \"And the curtain shall separate for you between the holy and the most holy\" (Exodus 26:33), and it is written: \"And you shall place the cover upon the Ark of the Testimony in the most holy place\" (Exodus 26:34), and there is no eating or drinking there, for none enters there except the High Priest on the Day of Atonement. And I saw in the Sifrei: Rabbi Yehudah ben Batera says: Consider gentiles who have surrounded the courtyard — from where do we learn that most holy sacrifices may be eaten even in the Hall (היכל)? Scripture says: \"In the most holy place you shall eat it.\" Now Rabbi Yehudah ben Batera raised this difficulty and established the verse for a situation of compulsion, and he expressed it with awe when he said \"may be eaten in the Hall,\" and it would seem that he should have said that they may be eaten even in the innermost sanctum if they surrounded the Hall. And according to the plain sense, we shall interpret <b>in the most holy place you shall eat it</b> — that you shall eat it with the sanctity of most holy things, for it said first: \"It is most holy to you and to your sons\" (Numbers 18:9), that you should not treat them in the manner of lighter sacrifices (קדשים קלים), but rather they shall be in your hands as most holy, and it returned and explained \"in the most holy place you shall eat it\" — that your eating of them shall be with strict sanctity. And since it already explained in the case of the meal offering and the sin offering the stringency that applies to their eating beyond lighter sacrifices in their place and their time, there was no need to elaborate here except to say that all of them are most holy in their eating, that your eating of them shall be with strict sanctity, not like lighter sacrifices. And the uses of the construct bet (בֵּית) are many, for the bet of the instrument holds both place and time as well as concept, and likewise: \"In toil you shall eat of it\" (Genesis 3:17)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:55.981192519Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}